+| ID (R14-GPT-NN) | Part (A\|B) | Severity | file:line | Evidence | Exact fix |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| R14-GPT-01 | A | P2 | release/tools/integrity.mjs:66–68 | **N12 / R15 A3 incomplete:** `if (bg[3] >= 1 && opaqueFrom[i] === 1) break` can stop before an ancestor’s blend mode; filters are never checked. Executed mock-DOM probes returned zero failures and zero unknowns for both an ancestor `mix-blend-mode:screen` above an opaque text background and text with `filter:opacity(.1)`. Browser rendering **UNVERIFIED**. These are ordinary compositing patterns. | Inspect the entire composed ancestor chain for unsupported compositing before stopping at an opaque background. Resolve supported effects; otherwise mark affected text unknown and make the JavaScript-off diagnostic INCOMPLETE. Add JS-on/off regressions for ancestor blending above an opaque child and filter opacity, with readable controls. |
+| R14-GPT-02 | A | P2 | release/tools/integrity.mjs:59; release/tools/integrity.mjs:93 | **N12/N13 coverage gap:** contrast skips elements without direct text nodes: `if (![...el.childNodes].some(...)) continue`. Native input values are also absent from content equivalence. An enabled `<input type="submit" value="Continue">` faded through opacity can therefore remain unreadable without JavaScript. Executed mock: scripted and faded input both produced `contrastChecked:1` from the unrelated heading, and integrity returned PASS. Browser rendering **UNVERIFIED**. | Include rendered native-control text—values, placeholders and selected-option text—in content equivalence and contrast coverage. Unsupported native painting must produce unknown coverage that blocks acceptance. Add regressions for an enabled submit input whose opacity is restored by JavaScript and for a control whose displayed value disappears without JavaScript. |
+| R14-GPT-03 | B | P2 | release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:69; release/tools/pre-handoff-check.mjs:225; release/tools/handoff-contract.mjs:139 | **R15 B1 enforcement incomplete:** the plan requires the “complete wireframe quality gate,” but declared-page iteration runs only under `phase === 'handoff'`. Wireframe manual coverage uses only `h.languages?.[0]?.lang`. Executed read-only contract check: two screens with nonexistent page paths and English/Arabic languages, with English-only manual records, returned `errors:[]`. | Add at Step 5 and in the contract: **“A wireframe PASS covers every declared desktop screen and locale. Each rendering must declare an existing page and receive the applicable structural/copy, accessibility, integrity and manual checks; missing coverage blocks structure approval.”** Implement phase-appropriate iteration and all-locale manual coverage. Add missing-page, second-screen failure and missing-second-locale regressions without requiring final visual artifacts or approval early. |
+| R14-GPT-04 | B | P2 | release/FANNAN-PLAN.md:75; release/references/handoff-contract.md:37; release/runtime/quality-checks.json:24 | “Start with the project's approved tokens and components” and per-component appearance/state specifications do not require a shared component/variant registry or a comparison across screens. Repeated controls can acquire inconsistent sizes, hierarchy and usage while satisfying the existing state inventory and craft records. | After Step 6’s opening paragraph, mirrored in the component contract, add: **“Maintain one canonical specification for each reused component: anatomy, semantic variants, sizes, token bindings, content constraints and composition/usage rules. Map every screen instance to that specification and the existing component where available. Compare equivalent instances across screens; unexplained differences fail.”** Add required manual ID `component-consistency`, judged structurally in wireframes and fully at handoff. |
