| R14-GPT-01 | A | P2 | release/tools/integrity.mjs:66–68 | **N12 / R15 A3 incomplete:** `if (bg[3] >= 1 && opaqueFrom[i] === 1) break` can stop before an ancestor’s blend mode; filters are never checked. Executed mock-DOM probes returned zero failures and zero unknowns for both an ancestor `mix-blend-mode:screen` above an opaque text background and text with `filter:opacity(.1)`. Browser rendering **UNVERIFIED**. These are ordinary compositing patterns. | Inspect the entire composed ancestor chain for unsupported compositing before stopping at an opaque background. Resolve supported effects; otherwise mark affected text unknown and make the JavaScript-off diagnostic INCOMPLETE. Add JS-on/off regressions for ancestor blending above an opaque child and filter opacity, with readable controls. |
| R14-GPT-02 | A | P2 | release/tools/integrity.mjs:59; release/tools/integrity.mjs:93 | **N12/N13 coverage gap:** contrast skips elements without direct text nodes: `if (![...el.childNodes].some(...)) continue`. Native input values are also absent from content equivalence. An enabled `<input type="submit" value="Continue">` faded through opacity can therefore remain unreadable without JavaScript. Executed mock: scripted and faded input both produced `contrastChecked:1` from the unrelated heading, and integrity returned PASS. Browser rendering **UNVERIFIED**. | Include rendered native-control text—values, placeholders and selected-option text—in content equivalence and contrast coverage. Unsupported native painting must produce unknown coverage that blocks acceptance. Add regressions for an enabled submit input whose opacity is restored by JavaScript and for a control whose displayed value disappears without JavaScript. |
